@model ReformedHistory.Views.Content.Confessions.Secret.SecretSection

<h2 id="@this.Model.Link">Chapter @this.Model.Index</h2>
<h3>@this.Model.Name</h3>
<br />

<div class="centerQuote">
    <span>
        The preaching of the Gospel is to be heard, and it is to be believed; and it is 
        to be held as beyond doubt that if you believe and are in Christ, you are elected. 
        For the Father has revealed unto us in Christ the eternal purpose of his predestination
    </span>
    <span>Heinrich Bullinger, The Second Helvetic Confession</span>
</div>
    
<ol>
    <li>
        To accomplish His eternal purpose of predestination, God chose for himself an elect, covenant people 
        in history to be united to Himself, so that He would be their God and they would be His people.
    </li>
    <li>
        The record of God's elect people can be followed in the scriptures through the history of Israel from
        Adam to Christ, which now culminates in the church as God's covenant people.
    </li>
    <li>
        Jesus is the promise that was anticipated by the saints in the old covenant, and Jesus is the
        means by which anyone who believes is elected in the new covenant. Jesus is the Annointed One, 
        the Holy One of God, the Elect One, and through union with Him, God's people are also elect.
    </li>
    <!--<li>
        God's covenant is his election, and His election fulfills His predestination; everyone in the
        covenant is elected in Christ and has received the gift of faith in baptism. This is a great 
        comfort, because we are assured that the promises of salvation are near to all of God's people.
    </li>-->
    <li>
        This great mystery of predestination and election is to be taught with the utmost care, and is always
        to be used for comforting the people of God with His great mercy, grace, and kindness; it is never to
        be the occasion for anxiety or speculation.
    </li>
    <li>
        If anyone is anxious about his election, look not to the secret things of God but to Christ, who is our
        election, and to the sacraments, which join us to Christ and sustain us in Christ. For the preaching
        of the gospel is not to be doubted, but believed with all faith, that 
        
        <i>"if you confess with your mouth
        that Jesus is Lord and believe in your heart that God raised him from the dead, you will be saved." </i>
    </li>
</ol>
<br />